(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_waves=[{id:"neet-2026r-q010",pn:10,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Waves",st:"Standing waves in organ pipes",q:"For sound waves, if the number of nodes for the 5th harmonic of an open-ended pipe is n and that for the 9th harmonic of the same pipe with one of its ends closed is m, the ratio n/m is",o:["9/5","1","3/5","5/9"],c:1,s:`Open organ pipe: number of nodes is the same as the number of harmonics.
So n = 5.
Closed organ pipe: only odd harmonics are present.
1st harmonic has 1 node, 3rd has 2 nodes, 5th has 3 nodes, 7th has 4 nodes, 9th has 5 nodes.
So m = 5.
n/m = 5/5 = 1.`},{id:"neet-2026-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Travelling wave phase",q:"For a travelling harmonic wave y(x, t) = 2.0cos2\u03C0(10t \u2212 0.0080x + 0.35), here x and y are in cm and t in s. The phase difference between oscillatory motion of two points separated by a distance of 0.5 m is:",o:["8\u03C0 rad","0.08\u03C0 rad","0.008\u03C0 rad","0.8\u03C0 rad"],c:3,s:`y(x,t) = 2.0cos2\u03C0(10t \u2212 0.008x + 0.35).
Total phase \u03C6 = 20\u03C0 \u2212 2\u03C0 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 x + 2\u03C0 \xD7 0.35.
Phase difference \u0394\u03C6 = k\u0394x, so |\u0394\u03C6| = 2\u03C0 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 \u0394x.
\u0394x = 0.5 m = 50 cm (printed as 100/2).
\u0394\u03C6 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 (100/2) = 8\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 = 0.8\u03C0 rad.`},{id:"neet-2025-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Waves",st:"Organ pipes, resonance",q:"A pipe open at both ends has a fundamental frequency f in air. The pipe is now dipped vertically in a water drum to half of its length. The fundamental frequency of the air column is now equal to:",o:["f/2","f","3f/2","2f"],c:1,s:`Fundamental frequency of an open pipe (open at both ends): f = v/2L ...(i)
Now, immersed in water, the open pipe behaves as a closed pipe of length L/2.
f\u2032 = v/(4(L/2)) = v/2L ...(ii)
So f = f\u2032.`,sfig:{w:270,h:390,alt:"Open pipe with its fundamental standing wave, and the pipe dipped half its length L/2 in a water drum with the closed-pipe fundamental",d:[["l",17,10,17,140],["l",93,10,93,140],["p","M17 12 C17 60 93 90 93 138"],["p","M93 12 C93 60 17 90 17 138"],["l",93,148,93,296],["l",157,148,157,296],["p","M97 152 C108 215 118 250 130 274 C142 250 152 215 157 152"],["t",36,203,"L/2"],["l",75,272,75,155,"b"],["l",15,258,15,380],["l",255,258,255,380],["l",15,380,255,380],["l",15,276,255,276,"d"],["l",20,297,250,297,"d"],["l",20,318,250,318,"d"],["l",20,339,250,339,"d"],["l",20,360,250,360,"d"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Organ pipes",q:"The ratio of frequencies of fundamental harmonic produced by an open pipe to that of closed pipe having the same length is:",o:["3 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1","1 : 3"],c:2,s:`Open pipe of length L: fundamental f\u2080 = v/2L.
Closed pipe of length L: fundamental f_c = v/4L.
f\u2080/f_c = (v/2L) \xD7 (4L/v) = 2/1.
So f\u2080 : f_c = 2 : 1.`},{id:"neet-2022-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Waves",st:"Speed of wave on a string",q:"If the initial tension on a stretched string is doubled, then the ratio of the initial and final speeds of a transverse wave along the string is",o:["\u221A2 : 1","1 : \u221A2","1 : 2","1 : 1"],c:1,s:`Wave speed on a string is v = \u221A(T/\u03BC).
Initial speed v\u1D62 = \u221A(T/\u03BC); final speed v_f = \u221A(2T/\u03BC).
v\u1D62/v_f = 1/\u221A2.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q048",pn:48,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Waves",st:"Speed of sound and temperature",q:"An organ pipe filled with a gas at 27\xB0C resonates at 400 Hz in its fundamental mode. If it is filled with the same gas at 90\xB0C, the resonance frequency at the same mode will be:",o:["512 Hz","420 Hz","440 Hz","484 Hz"],c:2,s:`Frequency f \u221D v \u221D \u221AT for the same pipe.
f\u2081/f\u2082 = \u221A(T\u2081/T\u2082) = \u221A(300/363) = 1/1.1.
So f\u2082 = 400 \xD7 1.1 = 440 Hz.`},{id:"neet-2020-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Waves",st:"Beats",q:"In a guitar, two strings A and B made of same material are slightly out of tune and produce beats of frequency 6 Hz. When tension in B is slightly decreased, the beat frequency increases to 7 Hz. If the frequency of A is 530 Hz, the original frequency of B will be :",o:["524 Hz","536 Hz","537 Hz","523 Hz"],c:0,s:`B is 530 \xB1 6 = 536 Hz or 524 Hz.
Frequency \u221D \u221Atension, so lowering the tension lowers the frequency of B.
If B were 536 Hz, the beat frequency would fall below 6 Hz.
If B is 524 Hz, going lower makes the beats rise to 7 Hz.
So B was 524 Hz.`},{id:"neet-2018-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Resonance tube",q:"A tuning fork is used to produce resonance in a glass tube. The length of the air column in this tube can be adjusted by a variable piston. At room temperature of 27\xB0C two successive resonance are produced at 20 cm and 73 cm of column length. If the frequency of the turning fork is 320 Hz, the velocity of sound in air at 27\xB0C is",o:["330 m/s","339 m/s","350 m/s","300 m/s"],c:1,s:`Two successive resonances differ by half a wavelength: l\u2082 \u2013 l\u2081 = (\u03BB)/(2).
(73 \u2013 20) cm = 0.53 m = (\u03BB)/(2), so \u03BB = 1.06 m.
v = f\u03BB = 320 \xD7 1.06 = 339 m/s.`},{id:"neet-2018-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Organ pipes",q:"The fundamental frequency in an open organ pipe is equal to the third harmonic of a closed organ pipe. If the length of the closed organ pipe is 20 cm, the length of the open organ pipe is",o:["13.2 cm","8 cm","12.5 cm","16 cm"],c:0,s:`Open pipe fundamental: f = (v)/(2l\u2081).
Closed pipe third harmonic (first overtone): f\u2083 = (3v)/(4l\u2082), with l\u2082 = 20 cm.
Equate: (v)/(2l\u2081) = (3v)/(4l\u2082), so l\u2081 = (2l\u2082)/(3) = (40)/(3) = 13.3 cm.
The nearest option is 13.2 cm.
Note: My working gives 13.3 cm; option 1 (13.2 cm) is the nearest and matches the printed key.`},{id:"neet-2017-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Doppler effect",q:"Two cars moving in opposite directions approach each other with speed of 22 m/s and 16.5 m/s respectively. The driver of the first car blows a horn having a frequency 400 Hz. The frequency heard by the driver of the second car is [velocity of sound 340 m/s] :",o:["448 Hz","350 Hz","361 Hz","411 Hz"],c:0,s:`For source and observer approaching: f' = f (v + vo)/(v \u2212 vs).
f' = 400 \xD7 (340 + 16.5)/(340 \u2212 22) = 400 \xD7 356.5/318.
f' \u2248 448 Hz.`},{id:"neet-2017-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Closed organ pipe",q:"The two nearest harmonics of a tube closed at one end and open at other end are 220 Hz and 260 Hz. What is the fundamental frequency of the system ?",o:["40 Hz","10 Hz","20 Hz","30 Hz"],c:2,s:`A closed pipe has only odd harmonics: f = n(v/4L) with n = 1, 3, 5, \u2026
Two nearest harmonics differ by 2 \xD7 fundamental, so 260 \u2212 220 = 40 = 2f0.
f0 = 20 Hz. (Check: 220 = 11 \xD7 20, 260 = 13 \xD7 20.)`},{id:"neet-2016b-q021",pn:21,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Waves",st:"Resonance in organ pipes",q:"The second overtone of an open organ pipe has the same frequency as the first overtone of a closed pipe L metre long. The length of the open pipe will be",o:["L","2L","L/2","4L"],c:1,s:`Open pipe: the second overtone is the third harmonic, f = 3v/(2l).
Closed pipe of length L: the first overtone is the third harmonic, f = 3v/(4L).
Equal frequencies: 3v/(2l) = 3v/(4L).
So 2l = 4L and l = 2L.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q022",pn:22,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Waves",st:"Beats",q:"Three sound waves of equal amplitudes have frequencies (n \u2013 1), n, (n + 1). They superimpose to give beats. The number of beats produced per second will be",o:["1","4","3","2"],c:3,s:`The beat frequency is the largest difference between the frequencies present.
(n + 1) \u2212 (n \u2212 1) = 2.
So 2 beats per second are heard.`},{id:"neet-2016-q151",pn:151,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Doppler effect and echo",q:"A siren emitting a sound of frequency 800 Hz moves away from an observer towards a cliff at a speed of 15 m s\u207B\xB9. Then, the frequency of sound that the observer hears in the echo reflected from the cliff is : (Take velocity of sound in air = 330 m s\u207B\xB9)",o:["800 Hz","838 Hz","885 Hz","765 Hz"],c:1,s:`The cliff is at rest, so it receives sound from a source moving towards it.
Frequency received by the cliff: n' = n\xB7v/(v - v_s).
The cliff reflects this frequency, and the observer (at rest) hears it unchanged.
n' = 800 \xD7 330/(330 - 15) = 800 \xD7 330/315 = 838 Hz.`,sfig:{w:330,h:145,alt:"Observer at left, source on a cart moving right with speed v\u209B towards a cliff (v_R = 0); sound n goes to the cliff and the echo returns to the observer.",d:[["l",12,129,221,129],["l",215,30,215,129],["l",215,36,221,30],["l",215,42,221,36],["l",215,48,221,42],["l",215,54,221,48],["l",215,60,221,54],["l",215,66,221,60],["l",215,72,221,66],["l",215,78,221,72],["l",215,84,221,78],["l",215,90,221,84],["l",215,96,221,90],["l",215,102,221,96],["l",215,108,221,102],["l",215,114,221,108],["l",215,120,221,114],["l",215,126,221,120],["c",33,103,8],["l",33,111,33,121],["l",23,116,43,116],["l",33,121,24,129],["l",33,121,43,129],["r",78,98,40,16],["t",98,110,"source","ms"],["c",86,122,7],["c",110,122,7],["l",118,106,138,106,"a"],["t",141,110,"v\u209B"],["t",157,126,"v\u209B"],["p","M213 77C160 48 70 55 37 89","a"],["p","M102 92C130 75 175 72 211 80","a"],["p","M92 90C75 84 58 85 43 92","a"],["t",193,20,"v\u1D63 = 0"],["t",28,78,"n\u2034"],["t",97,87,"n","m"],["t",47,106,"n\u2033"],["t",200,58,"n\u2033","m"],["t",204,94,"n\u2032","m"],["t",228,68,"n\u2033 ="],["t",260,73,"n","s"],["p","M272 50C268 62 268 76 272 88"],["p","M314 50C318 62 318 76 314 88"],["l",277,69,310,69],["t",293,63,"v","m"],["t",293,83,"v \u2212 v\u209B","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2016-q157",pn:157,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Resonance in a closed pipe",q:"An air column, closed at one end and open at the other, resonates with a tuning fork when the smallest length of the column is 50 cm. The next larger length of the column resonating with the same tuning fork is :",o:["100 cm","150 cm","200 cm","66.7 cm"],c:1,s:`A closed pipe resonates at lengths l = \u03BB/4, 3\u03BB/4, 5\u03BB/4 ...
Smallest: l\u2081 = \u03BB/4 = 50 cm.
Next: l\u2082 = 3\u03BB/4 = 3 \xD7 50 = 150 cm.`},{id:"neet-2016-q174",pn:174,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Wave on a hanging rope",q:"A uniform rope of length L and mass m\u2081 hangs vertically from a rigid support. A block of mass m\u2082 is attached to the free end of the rope. A transverse pulse of wavelength \u03BB\u2081 is produced at the lower end of the rope. The wavelength of the pulse when it reaches the top of the rope is \u03BB\u2082. The ratio \u03BB\u2082/\u03BB\u2081 is :",o:["\u221A((m\u2081 + m\u2082)/m\u2082)","\u221A(m\u2082/m\u2081)","\u221A((m\u2081 + m\u2082)/m\u2081)","\u221A(m\u2081/m\u2082)"],c:0,s:`Wave speed v = f\u03BB = \u221A(T/\u03BC), and the frequency f stays the same, so \u03BB \u221D \u221AT.
Tension at the bottom: T\u2081 = m\u2082g.
Tension at the top: T\u2082 = (m\u2081 + m\u2082)g.
\u03BB\u2082/\u03BB\u2081 = \u221A(T\u2082/T\u2081) = \u221A((m\u2081 + m\u2082)/m\u2082).`,sfig:{w:100,h:140,alt:"Rope of mass m\u2081 hanging from a rigid support with a block of mass m\u2082 at its lower end.",d:[["l",5,11,63,11],["l",9,11,5,6],["l",14,11,10,6],["l",19,11,15,6],["l",24,11,20,6],["l",29,11,25,6],["l",34,11,30,6],["l",39,11,35,6],["l",44,11,40,6],["l",49,11,45,6],["l",54,11,50,6],["l",59,11,55,6],["l",32,12,32,100],["l",38,12,38,100],["r",16,100,36,25,"f"],["t",44,60,"m\u2081"],["t",58,118,"m\u2082"]]}}]})();
